      "id": "S06-05-en",
      "locale": "en",
      "voice": "Charon",
      "sectionID": "worked-example",
      "spokenText": "In a hypothetical organism, chemical amount stays at zero point zero two milligrams while body mass increases from zero point zero one to zero point zero two kilograms on the same mass basis. Before growth, dividing chemical amount by body mass gives two milligrams per kilogram. Afterwards, the same amount divided by a larger body mass gives one milligram per kilogram. Concentration has halved, but chemical amount has not decreased. This illustrates growth dilution, not elimination of half the chemical or magnification between prey and predator. A real case needs observations distinguishing denominator changes from changes in chemical amount.",
